 CMAKE — THE GIST AND HOW TO USE IT
====================================

WHAT IS CMAKE?
---------------
CMake is NOT a compiler. It's a "build system generator."

You write one set of instructions (a file called CMakeLists.txt), and CMake
turns that into the actual build files for whatever tool you want to use:
- Makefiles (for `make` on Linux/macOS)
- Ninja build files
- Visual Studio project files (Windows)
- Xcode project files (macOS)

Why this matters: your project stays portable. One CMakeLists.txt can build
on Linux, Windows, and macOS without you rewriting anything.

THE BASIC WORKFLOW
--------------------
1. Write a CMakeLists.txt in your project folder describing what to build.
2. Run CMake to "configure" the project — this generates native build files
   in a separate build directory (keeps your source folder clean).
3. Run the actual build tool (or let CMake do it for you) to compile.

Commands (from your project root):

    mkdir build
    cd build
    cmake ..
    cmake --build .

Breaking that down:
- `cmake ..` reads the CMakeLists.txt in the parent folder and generates
  build files inside `build/`.
- `cmake --build .` invokes the underlying build tool (make, ninja, MSBuild,
  etc.) to actually compile — this way you don't need to remember the
  tool-specific command.

A MINIMAL CMakeLists.txt
--------------------------
    cmake_minimum_required(VERSION 3.10)
    project(MyApp)

    add_executable(MyApp main.cpp)

That's genuinely enough for a simple one-file program.

KEY CONCEPTS / COMMANDS YOU'LL ACTUALLY USE
----------------------------------------------
cmake_minimum_required(VERSION x.y)
    Sets the minimum CMake version needed. Always put this first.

project(Name)
    Names your project, sets up default compiler detection.

add_executable(target file1.cpp file2.cpp ...)
    Declares that these source files compile into an executable called
    "target".

add_library(mylib file1.cpp file2.cpp)
    Same idea, but builds a library (static by default) instead of an exe.

target_link_libraries(target mylib)
    Links "mylib" into "target". This is how you connect libraries to
    executables (or other libraries).

target_include_directories(target PUBLIC include/)
    Tells the compiler where to find header files.

set(VAR value)
    Defines a variable, e.g. set(CMAKE_CXX_STANDARD 17)

find_package(SomeLib REQUIRED)
    Locates an already-installed library (like Boost, OpenCV, etc.) on the
    system so you can link against it.

add_subdirectory(some_folder)
    Pulls in another folder that has its own CMakeLists.txt — used for
    multi-folder / multi-module projects.

PUBLIC vs PRIVATE vs INTERFACE (quick note)
-----------------------------------------------
When linking or including things, you'll see keywords like PUBLIC, PRIVATE,
INTERFACE. They control who "inherits" that requirement:
- PRIVATE: only this target uses it.
- PUBLIC: this target uses it AND anything linking to this target inherits
  it too.
- INTERFACE: this target doesn't use it directly, but anything linking to
  it does.

Rule of thumb for beginners: start with PRIVATE unless you know consumers
of your library also need that dependency.

A SLIGHTLY BIGGER EXAMPLE
----------------------------
Project structure:

    myproject/
      CMakeLists.txt
      main.cpp
      mathlib/
        add.cpp
        add.h

CMakeLists.txt:

    cmake_minimum_required(VERSION 3.10)
    project(MyProject)
    set(CMAKE_CXX_STANDARD 17)

    add_library(mathlib mathlib/add.cpp)
    target_include_directories(mathlib PUBLIC mathlib)

    add_executable(MyProject main.cpp)
    target_link_libraries(MyProject PRIVATE mathlib)

Build it the same way as before:

    mkdir build && cd build
    cmake ..
    cmake --build .

COMMON GOTCHAS
----------------
- Always build "out of source" (in a separate build/ folder). Building
  directly in your source folder makes a mess and is hard to clean up.
- If you change CMakeLists.txt, just re-run `cmake ..` (or `cmake --build .`
  usually re-triggers configure automatically) — you don't need to delete
  the build folder unless something's badly broken, in which case wiping
  build/ and starting over is the standard fix.
- CMake versions matter: features get added over time, so an old
  cmake_minimum_required can block you from using newer syntax.
- On Windows with Visual Studio, `cmake ..` generates a .sln file you can
  open directly, or you can still use `cmake --build .`.

